 "literal": "My legs indeed have lost their speed, my eyes indeed / looking and looking, have lost their brightness / than the stars of the wide, vast sky / surely, many indeed are the others in this world.",
 "translation": "My legs have lost their strength from walking. My eyes have lost their brightness from searching and searching. Yet, those who are strangers in this world are surely more numerous than the stars in the wide, vast sky.",
 "modern": "என் கால்கள் நடந்து நடந்து வலிமை இழந்துவிட்டன. என் கண்கள் தேடித் தேடிப் பார்த்துத் தம் ஒளியை இழந்துவிட்டன. அகன்ற பெரிய வானத்தில் உள்ள விண்மீன்களைக் காட்டிலும், இவ்வுலகத்தில் உள்ள பிற மனிதர்கள் மிகவும் பலராவர்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the desert landscape (pālai tiṇai), where a foster-mother laments her futile search for her eloped daughter and her lover. She expresses her physical and emotional exhaustion, stating that her legs can no longer walk and her eyes have grown dim from constant searching. The vastness of her despair is highlighted by comparing the indifferent strangers she encounters to the countless stars in the night sky. Despite the world being crowded with people, none of them are the loved ones she seeks, intensifying her sense of isolation. The key terms 'paritappal' (exhaustion of legs) and 'piṟar' (strangers) underscore the theme of separation and loneliness.",
